export default "Natural remedy claims for period cramps range from evidence-backed to scientifically implausible. The distinction matters when you are in pain. Investing time in something with no mechanism or trial evidence costs you options that do work.\n\nHere is an honest ranking.\n\n## Tier 1: Strong Evidence\n\n### Heat Therapy\n\n**Evidence:** One RCT comparing continuous low-level heat (38-40 degrees C applied to lower abdomen for 8 hours) to ibuprofen 400mg found heat equivalent to ibuprofen for pain relief in primary dysmenorrhea. A separate trial found combined heat and ibuprofen superior to either alone.\n\n**Mechanism:** Vasodilation counteracts the prostaglandin-driven vasoconstriction that causes ischemic uterine pain. It also relaxes smooth muscle directly.\n\n**How to use it:**\n- Place heating pad or adhesive heat patch on lower abdomen, not lower back\n- Temperature: warm to the touch but not hot (38-40 degrees C)\n- Duration: continuous application is significantly more effective than brief sessions\n- Disposable adhesive heat patches (ThermaCare, store brands) maintain temperature for 8 hours\n\n**Limitation:** Does not address systemic prostaglandin effects. Nausea, diarrhea, and headaches from cramping require NSAIDs for best relief.\n\n---\n\n### Ginger\n\n**Evidence:** Three small RCTs comparing ginger to ibuprofen or placebo for primary dysmenorrhea found ginger reduced pain severity comparably to ibuprofen 400mg.\n\n**Important dose details from the trials:**\n- 500mg dried ginger powder capsules\n- 3 times daily\n- Started 2 days before expected menstruation\n- Continued through day 3 of the period\n\nThis is not the amount of ginger you would consume from ginger tea or cooking. A cup of ginger tea contains roughly 250mg of ginger root. The effective dose in trials was 1500mg daily in divided doses.\n\n**Mechanism:** Gingerols and shogaols (the active compounds) inhibit cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, the same pathways NSAIDs block. The anti-inflammatory effect is real. The potency is lower than pharmaceutical NSAIDs.\n\n**Practical note:** Capsules of standardized dried ginger are easier to dose consistently than fresh ginger, tea, or cooking. Look for products listing 500mg dried ginger root (not extract).\n\n---\n\n## Tier 2: Good Mechanism, Consistent Positive Trials\n\n### Magnesium (Preventive)\n\n**Evidence:** Multiple RCTs for PMS and dysmenorrhea specifically. A 1995 double-blind placebo-controlled trial found magnesium reduced dysmenorrhea significantly versus placebo. Several subsequent studies confirm the effect.\n\n**Mechanism:** Magnesium reduces prostaglandin E2 synthesis and acts as a calcium antagonist in smooth muscle, reducing uterine muscle contraction intensity. Deficiency is associated with worse cramping.\n\n**How to use it correctly:**\n- Take consistently every day, not just during cramps (preventive, not acute)\n- Magnesium glycinate or bisglycinate: best absorbed, minimal GI side effects\n- 200-360mg elemental magnesium daily (check labels; magnesium oxide is poorly absorbed)\n- Assess after 2-3 cycles, not after one cycle\n\n**Magnesium oxide:** widely sold, cheapest, but absorption is only 4% versus 23-40% for glycinate forms. Effectively less than 15mg elemental magnesium absorbed per 400mg tablet. This is why \"I tried magnesium and it did not work\" often means inadequate absorption from the wrong form.\n\n---\n\n### Omega-3 Fatty Acids (Preventive)\n\n**Evidence:** Several RCTs for dysmenorrhea show significant pain reduction with EPA+DHA supplementation versus placebo. A 2011 trial found omega-3s more effective than ibuprofen for pain relief after 3 months of supplementation.\n\n**Mechanism:** EPA and DHA compete with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase enzymes, shifting prostaglandin production toward less-inflammatory variants (PGE3 and PGI3 versus PGE2 and PGF2alpha). This reduces the inflammatory prostaglandin load at the source.\n\n**Timeline:** 6-8 weeks minimum to shift membrane fatty acid composition; full effect at 3 months.\n\n**Dose:** 1-2g EPA+DHA combined daily. Check labels for actual EPA+DHA content, not total fish oil.\n\n**What does not count:** Flaxseed ALA converts to EPA/DHA at only 1-10% efficiency. For dysmenorrhea, you need EPA and DHA directly, from fatty fish, fish oil, or algae-based DHA+EPA.\n\n---\n\n### TENS (Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation)\n\n**Evidence:** Multiple RCTs for high-frequency TENS in primary dysmenorrhea, with consistent findings of reduced pain intensity versus sham TENS.\n\n**How to use:**\n- Electrode pad placement: lower abdomen, bilateral, above pubic bone\n- High frequency (50-120 Hz), this is the evidence-supported setting\n- Sessions of 30-45 minutes; relief often extends beyond the session\n- Intensity: should produce a noticeable buzzing or tingling sensation without pain\n\n**Cost:** Standard TENS units $40-$100. Period-specific brands (Livia, OVIRA) $150-$250. The period-specific devices are TENS units with preset settings. A standard unit at lower cost works equivalently.\n\n---\n\n## Tier 3: Limited Evidence, Plausible Mechanism\n\n### Exercise\n\n**Evidence:** Several small trials show aerobic exercise reduces dysmenorrhea severity. The effect is most consistent for light-to-moderate exercise (walking, yoga) rather than high intensity.\n\n**Mechanism:** Endorphin release (broad pain modulation), improved uterine blood flow (reduces ischemia), and reduced prostaglandin sensitivity. Exercise also reduces cortisol, which influences prostaglandin production.\n\n**Practical approach:** A 20-30 minute walk during crampy days. Yoga, particularly poses that open the hips and decompress the lower abdomen, has some trial evidence.\n\n### Vitamin D\n\n**Evidence:** Observational associations between vitamin D deficiency and worse dysmenorrhea. One small intervention trial found reduced pain severity with vitamin D supplementation in deficient participants.\n\n**Caveat:** The effect may be entirely attributable to correcting deficiency rather than supplementation per se. Test your level. If deficient, correcting it is worth doing regardless of dysmenorrhea.\n\n### Acupuncture\n\n**Evidence:** Multiple small trials, systematic reviews with inconsistent findings. Some show benefit over sham acupuncture; some do not. The evidence suggests a real effect but the quality and magnitude are uncertain.\n\n**Practical consideration:** Access and cost. If you have access to acupuncture and are interested, it is a reasonable option. Do not pay significant money for it based on the current evidence quality.\n\n---\n\n## What to Skip\n\n**Essential oils:** No RCT evidence for period cramp relief. One small trial used aromatherapy applied to the abdomen and found reduced pain versus unscented oil. This is essentially a skin-applied warm oil massage, and the comparison was to unscented oil, not to established treatments.\n\n**Castor oil packs:** No controlled evidence. Warm castor oil packs applied to the abdomen may provide some comfort from the warmth itself (equivalent to a lower-temperature heating pad), but the castor oil specifically has no established mechanism for cramping.\n\n**Crystal therapy, herbal vaginal steaming:** No biological mechanism or trial evidence.\n\n**\"Cramp bark\" supplements:** Some anti-spasmodic properties in animal studies; no human RCT evidence for dysmenorrhea specifically.\n\n**Raspberry leaf tea:** Traditional use; no clinical trial evidence for cramping specifically.\n\n---\n\n## A Practical Protocol\n\nFor acute cramp relief without NSAIDs:\n1. Heat patch on lower abdomen, continuous\n2. Ginger capsules 500mg times 3 (if started before the period)\n3. TENS device if available\n\nFor prevention over multiple cycles:\n1. Magnesium glycinate 300mg daily\n2. Omega-3 EPA+DHA 1-2g daily\n3. Anti-inflammatory diet (reduce processed foods and omega-6 oils)\n\nFor best results: combine preventive supplements with heat and/or ginger for acute relief. The preventive approaches reduce severity. The acute approaches address what remains.\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nTracking which interventions you used and how severe cramps were by day gives you your own controlled experiment over time. After 3 cycles using magnesium consistently and 3 cycles without, you have personal data on whether it works for you specifically. That is more valuable than any population average.\n## Medical Context\n\nThis page is educational and is not medical advice. Use it to prepare questions and track patterns; diagnosis, treatment decisions, supplement dosing, and medication use should be reviewed with a qualified clinician, especially if pain is severe, bleeding is heavy, cycles change suddenly, pregnancy is possible, or you have a known condition.";
